<title>NETHERLANDS: Dutch February consumer prices rise 2.2 pct yr/yr.</title>
<headline>Dutch February consumer prices rise 2.2 pct yr/yr.</headline>
<dateline>VOORBURG, Netherlands 1997-03-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Dutch consumer price index (CPI) rose a provisional 0.2 percent in February from January, giving a 2.2 percent year-on-year rise, the Central Bureau of Statistics (CBS) said.  </p>
<p>DUTCH CONSUMER PRICE INFLATION   Feb 1997    Jan 1997   Feb 1996</p>
<p> Monthly CPI pct change		 0.2	  0.3	  0.3</p>
<p> Year-on-year CPI pct change	  2.2	  2.3	  1.8</p>
<p> Index (base 1990)		    118.2	118.0	115.7</p>
<p>NOTES - Economists had expected the February CPI to rise between 2.1 percent and 2.6 percent year-on-year.  </p>
<p>The detailed index figures were:</p>
<p>					    Weighting   Feb 1997  Jan 1997</p>
<p> Food, drink, tobacco			 180     110.5     111.7</p>
<p> Clothes, shoes				  72	90.5	87.0</p>
<p> Rent, heating, lighting		    261     132.5     132.5</p>
<p> Furniture, household appliances etc.    80     107.0     106.8</p>
<p> Medical care				     6     119.5     119.5</p>
<p> Transport, communication		   144     119.9     119.9</p>
<p> Leisure, education			   107     109.5     109.0</p>
<p> Other goods, services			113     117.1     116.4</p>
<p> Consumer taxes, govt. services	    37     154.7     154.7</p>
<p>Clothing and shoes have become more expensive as a result of the introduction of summer collections, the CBS said, rising by an average of four percent in February.</p>
<p>The CBS said the price of coffee and eggs had also risen in February.</p>
<p>However, vegetables were about 15 percent cheaper in February compared to January, because of milder weather.</p>
<p>-- Amsterdam Newsroom +31 20 570 8500</p>
